from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("sub", "8.4 One lake, and who is cast into it"),
("p", "The lake of fire is one place with one revealed character, and the human lost who are cast into it remain under its punishment for ever. The conclusion is not the statement of any single verse, and the volume does not present it as one. It is built in six steps, and each step is marked, so that the reader can see which parts are the text’s own words and which are the conclusion drawn from them. The conclusion is Inferred, and Inferred here does not mean uncertain: a conclusion that several passages require together is firmly established, even when no one verse says the whole of it in one sentence."),
("sub", "Step one: the nature of the lake"),
("p", "Revelation 20:10 gives the lake its character. The devil “was cast into the lake of fire and brimstone, where the beast and the false prophet are, and shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever”. The sentence names a place, a fire and a brimstone, and then says what is done to those in it: they are tormented, without a pause (“day and night”) and without a term (“for ever and ever”). That the devil is cast into the lake and tormented there for ever and ever is Stated. That the beast and the false prophet are included in the plural statement of torment is shown by the grammar and the context (section 8.2). The lake is therefore described, in the verse that first says what happens in it, as a place of enduring torment, and it is described so before any human dead are said to be cast into it at the throne."),
("sub", "Step two: one lake, not several"),
("p", "The book does not speak of several fires. It speaks of one lake, and it names it in the same way each time it speaks of it. The table sets the passages side by side."),
("ctbl", "How Revelation names the lake.", [
    ("Passage", "How the lake is named", "Who is in it or is cast into it"),
    ("Revelation 19:20", "“a lake of fire burning with brimstone”", "The beast and the false prophet, cast alive"),
    ("Revelation 20:10", "“the lake of fire and brimstone”", "The devil, cast in where the beast and the false prophet are; tormented day and night for ever and ever"),
    ("Revelation 20:14", "“the lake of fire”, “the second death”", "Death and Hell, cast in"),
    ("Revelation 20:15", "“the lake of fire”", "Whosoever was not found written in the book of life, cast in"),
    ("Revelation 21:8", "“the lake which burneth with fire and brimstone: which is the second death”", "The fearful, unbelieving and the rest named there, who have their part in it"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("p", "Four things in the table are Stated. The pair “fire and brimstone” stands in 19:20, 20:10 and 21:8. The word “lake” is the name in every passage, and the Greek names it with the definite article in each of the four later places, as the one already introduced. The article does not by itself prove that the lake is the same; the case rests on the repeated designation and the surrounding context together. Each passage uses an inflected form of the same Greek verb for casting, βάλλω (19:20, 20:10, 20:14 and 20:15), so that the beast and the false prophet, the devil, Death and Hell, and the human dead are all said to have been cast into it by the one verb, in forms that differ only by number and tense. And the identification “This is the second death” in 20:14 and “which is the second death” in 21:8 is one identification made twice. That these are one lake and not different punishments with similar imagery is shown by the repetition: the book introduces no second lake, no second fire and no second name, and it never marks a change of place between one passage and the next. The unity of the lake is shown by the context and is not Stated in a single sentence."),
("sub", "Step three: the lake keeps its character when human beings are cast into it"),
("p", "Nothing in the text says that the lake changes when human beings are cast into it, and the text says three things that point the other way. First, 21:8 comes after 20:15, and it describes the lake in the present tense as the lake “which burneth with fire and brimstone”. After the human dead have been cast in, the lake is described as it was described before. It is not described as emptied, as burned out or as ended. That is Stated. Second, 21:8 does not say that the fearful and the unbelieving are consumed by the lake. It says that they “shall have their part” in it, and a part is a share in something that is there (section 8.3). Third, the sentence of 20:15 is the execution of a judgment, and the lake is its penalty. A penalty is known by what is done in it, and the book has already said what is done in this one. That the lake does not change its character when human beings are cast in is shown by the context, since the text signals no change, and it is Inferred as the conclusion the three observations require."),
("p", "There is a further point. The volume reads the beast and the false prophet as persons, and Revelation says of the beast that his number “is the number of a man” (Revelation 13:18). If the beast and the false prophet are human beings, as the volume reads them, then Revelation 20:10 already puts human beings in the lake under torment day and night for ever and ever, and the step from the first occupants to the later ones is a step within one kind of person. The volume marks that reading as Inferred, and it does not rest the argument on it. The argument stands without it."),
("sub", "Step four: the human evidence of Revelation 14:9–11"),
("p", "Revelation 14:9–11 is the passage that speaks of human beings under this punishment in its own words. The third angel says of “any man” who worships the beast and his image and receives his mark that “he shall be tormented with fire and brimstone in the presence of the holy angels, and in the presence of the Lamb”, that “the smoke of their torment ascendeth up for ever and ever”, and that “they have no rest day nor night”. These are human beings, and what is Stated of them is torment with fire and brimstone, torment before the angels and the Lamb, a smoke of their torment that goes up for ever and ever, and no rest day nor night."),
("ctbl", "Revelation 14:9–11 and 20:10 side by side.", [
    ("Element", "Revelation 14:9–11 (human worshippers)", "Revelation 20:10 (the devil, the beast and the false prophet)"),
    ("The fire", "“fire and brimstone”", "“the lake of fire and brimstone”"),
    ("The torment", "“he shall be tormented”; “the smoke of their torment”", "“shall be tormented”"),
    ("The continuance", "“no rest day nor night”", "“day and night”"),
    ("The duration", "“ascendeth up for ever and ever”, said of the smoke of their torment", "“for ever and ever”, said of the torment"),
  ], [0.18, 0.41, 0.41]),
("p", "The two passages use different inflected forms of the same Greek verb of torment, βασανίζω, the same pair of fire and brimstone, the same “day” and “night” for its continuance and the same “for ever and ever” for its length. The first passage says what happens to human beings and does not name the lake. The second names the lake and says how long the torment in it lasts. That the two passages share these terms is Stated. That they describe one torment, and that the torment of the human worshippers has the length that 20:10 gives to the torment in the lake, is Inferred from the shared terms and from the absence of any second kind of torment in the book. The inference is firm, because the alternative is to hold that Revelation describes two different torments in identical words and gives no sign of the difference. The volume does not read the phrase “for ever and ever” of 14:11 as set on the no-rest clause (section 8.1). It reads it as the smoke’s, and it reads the lasting of the torment itself from 20:10."),
("sub", "Step five: what the second death contributes"),
("p", "The name “the second death” does not turn the lake into extinction, because the book has already said what the lake is. The name is given to a place already described. 20:14 says “This is the second death”, and 21:8 says of the lake that burneth with fire and brimstone, “which is the second death”. The clause identifies the lake as the second death, and it does not define the lake by the word death. The lake is the subject, and the book has described it, in 20:10 and in 21:8, as a place of burning and torment. The name therefore takes its meaning from the lake and not the lake from the name."),
("p", "What the name does contribute is stated by the book. It is a second death after the first, and it is where those not found written in the book of life are sent (Revelation 20:15). It is something that hurts and has power: “He that overcometh shall not be hurt of the second death” (Revelation 2:11), and “on such the second death hath no power” (Revelation 20:6). It is final, and it is the opposite of the life of the city, into which “there shall in no wise enter” anything that defileth (Revelation 21:27). Section 8.3 showed that in Revelation those held in the first death continue as persons who are raised and judged, so the word death does not carry cessation into the lake. The second death contributes finality, separation from life and hurt. It does not contribute ceasing. That is Stated in the clauses quoted and Inferred in the conclusion about the word."),
("sub", "Step six: the strongest objection"),
("p", "The objection. Revelation 20:10 says in so many words that the devil, the beast and the false prophet are tormented for ever and ever. Revelation 20:15 says only that the human dead are cast into the lake. The first sentence states a duration and a torment, and the second states neither. The character of the lake in the first cannot be carried over to human beings in the second, and a reader who does so is saying more than the verse says."),
("p", "The reply. The carrying over is legitimate, because the text itself does it: it names one lake, it describes it in the same terms before and after the human dead are cast in, and it states the torment of human beings in the same terms in 14:9–11 and gives the human lost their part in the burning lake in 21:8. The objection is right about what 20:15 says on its own and wrong about what the book says. The volume does not claim that 20:15 states a torment of the human dead for ever and ever in one sentence. It claims that Revelation, read as a whole, requires that conclusion."),
("p", "The decisive distinction is between a verse and a book. A verse that says a person is cast into a place does not need to repeat what the book has already said the place is. 20:10 has said what the lake is. 20:15 says who goes into it. A reader who accepts the first and wants the second to say it again has asked of a verse what the book has already supplied. Four considerations show that the extension is required and not merely allowed. The first is that the lake is one, and the repetition of its name, its fire and its brimstone shows it (step two). The second is that the text signals no change in the lake when the human dead enter it, and 21:8 describes it afterwards as burning (step three). The third is that human beings are said to be tormented in fire and brimstone with no rest day nor night, in the terms of 20:10 (step four). The fourth is that the alternative requires two claims that no text makes: that the same lake torments its first occupants for ever and ends its later ones, and that the casting of 20:15 is a different act from the casting of 19:20, 20:10 and 20:14, in the same word. A reading that requires two unstated claims is not the plain sense of the book."),
("p", "Outside Revelation the Lord’s own words go the same way, and the volume marks the point as corroboration and not as the ground. In the judgment of the nations the King sends the goats away into “everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matthew 25:41), and the passage closes with the persons going into “everlasting punishment” (Matthew 25:46). The human lost are sent to the fire prepared for the devil. The volume reads that fire as the same destination that Revelation names as the lake, and marks the identification as Inferred, because Matthew 25 does not use the word lake. It is spoken to Israel in the Kingdom ministry and is used as a witness to the fate of the wicked, and not as a word to the Body."),
("p", "What is conceded. The devil is not a man, and the volume has said so (section 8.2); 20:10 states the torment of the devil, and of the beast and the false prophet by the plural, and does not state it of the human dead who come later. No verse says in one sentence that the human dead of 20:15 are tormented for ever and ever. That is why the conclusion is marked Inferred. Verdict: the character of the lake that 20:10 reveals governs the human lost cast into it, because the book names one lake, describes it unchanged after they enter it, and states their torment in the same terms; the objection shows that the conclusion is cumulative, and it does not show that the conclusion is unsupported."),
("ctbl", "The steps of the argument and the marks.", [
    ("Step", "What is held", "The mark"),
    ("1", "The lake is a place of torment day and night for ever and ever", "Stated of the devil (20:10); the beast and the false prophet included, shown by the grammar and the context"),
    ("2", "The lake of 19:20, 20:10, 20:14, 20:15 and 21:8 is one lake", "Names and verb Stated; unity shown by the context"),
    ("3", "The lake keeps its character when human beings are cast into it", "21:8 describes it as burning, Stated; no signal of change, shown by the context; the conclusion Inferred"),
    ("4", "Human worshippers of the beast are tormented with fire and brimstone and have no rest day nor night", "Stated (14:9–11); the shared terms with 20:10 Stated; one torment, Inferred"),
    ("5", "The second death names the lake and does not define it as extinction", "The identification Stated (20:14; 21:8); the conclusion about the word Inferred"),
    ("6", "The human lost cast into the lake remain under its punishment for ever and do not cease to exist", "Inferred, firmly, from steps 1 to 5"),
  ], [0.1, 0.5, 0.4]),
("sub", "8.5 Revelation 20:11–15 read for what it establishes of final punishment"),
("p", "The throne scene is read here for one thing: what it says about the punishment that follows. The seer writes, “And I saw a great white throne, and him that sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away; and there was found no place for them” (Revelation 20:11). The dead “small and great” stand before God, books are opened, and “the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works” (Revelation 20:12). The sea, Death and Hades give up their dead, and “they were judged every man according to their works” (Revelation 20:13). Then the sentence is carried out: “And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death. And whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the lake of fire” (Revelation 20:14, 15)."),
("p", "Three things follow from the text, and they are the volume’s only claims from it. The first is that the judgment is individual and according to works: each is judged “every man according to their works” (Revelation 20:13). The second is that the sentence is a casting into the lake, which is an act done to persons. The third is that the passage does not say that the lake is emptied or that those cast into it cease. It says that they are cast in, and the preceding section has shown what the book says of the lake."),
("p", "A judgment that is according to works is a judgment in which the persons are not all treated alike. The Lord said as much in the days of His ministry to Israel. Of Tyre and Sidon He said that it would be “more tolerable” for them “at the day of judgment” than for the cities that saw His works (Matthew 11:22), and of Sodom the same (Matthew 11:24). Of the servant who knew his lord’s will and did it not He said that he “shall be beaten with many stripes”, and of him who knew not, “few stripes” (Luke 12:47, 48). Of the scribes and Pharisees He said, “ye shall receive the greater damnation” (Matthew 23:14), and the letter to the Hebrews speaks of “sorer punishment” for him who has trodden under foot the Son of God (Hebrews 10:29). These words were spoken to Israel and to the Hebrew readers, and they are witnesses of what Scripture reveals of the day of judgment and not words to the Body. They state that the punishment is graded."),
("p", "The grading of the judgment shows that the punishment is described as an infliction with a content and a measure, and not as a ceasing. A punishment which can be more tolerable or less, which is a measure of stripes, and which is said to be “greater” and “sorer”, is described as something the persons have. That is shown by the context. The writer who holds that the lost cease to exist grants the grading and says that it is the measure of suffering before the end. The volume grants that this reading is possible and that degrees alone do not decide the question; the passage does not say that the content is never completed, and the descriptions the Scripture uses are the descriptions of an infliction."),
("sub", "8.6 Who stands at the great white throne"),
("p", "The question is a natural one, and the passage raises it. Who are “the dead, small and great”? The volume asks it because its own subject requires an answer. The people whom the text says are cast into the lake are the people whose punishment the volume describes, and it is fair to ask who they are. The volume’s answer is that the company is the rest of the dead of verse 5, argued from the sequence of the chapter, and each step is marked."),
("sub", "The company at the throne"),
("p", "Revelation 20:12 does not call the company by a name beyond “the dead, small and great”. Verse 13 adds that the sea, Death and Hades give up their dead, which says where they were, and that they are judged “every man according to their works”. The identification of this company with “the rest of the dead” is a conclusion drawn from the sequence of the chapter. In verse 4 the seer sees thrones and those who sat upon them, and “the souls of them that were beheaded for the witness of Jesus, and for the word of God”, who “lived and reigned with Christ a thousand years”. Verse 5 says, “But the rest of the dead lived not again until the thousand years were finished. This is the first resurrection.” The thousand years end in verse 7, and the throne stands in verse 11. The dead who stand before the throne are the dead who lived again when the thousand years were finished, and this is the group verse 5 calls the rest of the dead. The volume marks this as shown by the context and Inferred from the sequence. It is not Stated in verse 12. Verse 12 does not say “the rest”."),
("sub", "Who is excluded, and why"),
("p", "Two companies are not at the throne for the judgment that ends in the lake. The first is the company of the first resurrection. Of them the text says, “Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection: on such the second death hath no power” (Revelation 20:6). The second death is the lake (Revelation 20:14), and those on whom it has no power are not cast into it. Because the second death has no power over the first-resurrection company, they cannot be included among those condemned to the lake. The volume therefore distinguishes them from the company subjected to the throne’s final sentence. This is Stated in verse 6 for the power of the second death, and it is shown by the context for their absence from the throne."),
("p", "The second is the Body of Christ. The Body’s accounting is set elsewhere: “we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ” (Romans 14:10), and “we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ; that every one may receive the things done in his body, according to that he hath done, whether it be good or bad” (2 Corinthians 5:10). That is a different court, with a different seat, a different people and a different end, and its nature is the subject of Volume 5 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Separate Courts. That the Body does not appear in the scenes of Revelation, and that the great white throne is not its judgment, is a conclusion the volume draws from the audience and setting of Revelation as a whole. It is marked Inferred, and it is not established by Romans 14:10 and 2 Corinthians 5:10 alone, which show only that the Body’s accounting is set at another seat."),
("sub", "Works and the book of life"),
("p", "The passage speaks of two kinds of record, and the volume keeps them apart. The books are opened, and “the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works” (Revelation 20:12). That is one function: the books supply the record by which the dead are judged by their works. “And another book was opened, which is the book of life”: that is a second book, and the verse names it separately. Its function is stated at the end: “whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the lake of fire” (Revelation 20:15). The criterion of the casting is not being found written."),
("p", "The text does not say how the two books are related, and the volume does not make it say. It does not say that a person judged by works is found written in the book of life. It does not say that no one at the throne is. It says that the dead are judged by their works and that whoever is not found written is cast in. Elsewhere the book speaks of the book of life in other terms: “he that overcometh, the same shall be clothed in white raiment; and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life” (Revelation 3:5), and the new Jerusalem receives only “they which are written in the Lamb’s book of life” (Revelation 21:27). These are statements of what the book of life is for. They tell nothing of the relation between the books and the book of life at the throne. The volume holds both functions intact."),
("sub", "Those who believed in the thousand years"),
("p", "One question is left, and the volume asks it plainly: does anyone who believed during the thousand years stand at the throne, and how? The text of chapter 20 names the thousand years’ people in one place. When Satan is loosed, he goes out “to deceive the nations which are in the four quarters of the earth, Gog and Magog”, and they “went up on the breadth of the earth, and compassed the camp of the saints about, and the beloved city: and fire came down from God out of heaven, and devoured them” (Revelation 20:8, 9). So there are nations on the earth at the end of the thousand years, and there is a camp of saints. The prophets describe the age in which they live: “the child shall die an hundred years old; but the sinner being an hundred years old shall be accursed” (Isaiah 65:20), and “every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, the LORD of hosts” (Zechariah 14:16). The new earth has “the nations of them which are saved” (Revelation 21:24)."),
("p", "The prophetic passages describe mortal life and death within the kingdom setting, surviving nations worshipping the King, and saved nations in the New Earth. They do not explicitly explain when kingdom believers who die are raised or whether any appear at the Great White Throne. The volume has asked the question and examined these passages, and the text does not answer it. The volume leaves it open and does not fill the gap. Nothing in the argument about the lake depends on the answer."),
("sub", "The earlier volume"),
("p", "Volume 5 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Separate Courts, set the great white throne among the courts of Scripture and left two points open on purpose: whether anyone who believed during the thousand years stands at the throne, and how the works and the book of life relate. This volume examines those questions because its own question requires it. Where it goes further than that volume, as in the identification of the throne company with the rest of the dead, it says so and marks the step as drawn from the sequence of the chapter. Where it does not, as in the thousand-year question and the relation of the books, it stands where that volume stood. It does not say that the earlier volume was wrong, and it does not rewrite its words."),
("ctbl", "Who stands at the great white throne, and what is claimed.", [
    ("Question", "The volume’s position", "The mark"),
    ("Who stands", "The dead, small and great, whom the sea, Death, and Hades give up", "Stated (20:12, 13)"),
    ("They are the rest of the dead of 20:5", "Drawn from the order of the chapter; verse 12 does not say so", "shown by the context; Inferred"),
    ("The first-resurrection company is not cast into the lake", "The second death has no power over it", "Stated (20:6)"),
    ("The Body is not judged here", "Its accounting is the judgment seat of Christ; the Body’s absence from Revelation’s scenes is the volume’s conclusion from the book’s audience and setting", "Inferred (Romans 14:10; 2 Corinthians 5:10 set its accounting at another seat)"),
    ("The books and the book of life", "Two functions, kept apart; no relation drawn", "Stated (20:12, 15)"),
    ("Those who believed in the thousand years", "Not answered by the text; left open", "Left open"),
  ], [0.3, 0.45, 0.25]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Revelation names its audience and describes events still to come; its warnings to the worshippers of the beast are witnesses to the fate of the wicked and not words to the Body.",
    "Revelation 14:9–11 does not rest on the smoke. It says that the worshippers of the beast are tormented and have no rest day nor night, and it sets that beside the rest of those who die in the Lord.",
    "The torment words of the book describe what is suffered by someone who is aware of it.",
    "The beast and the false prophet were cast alive into the lake, the devil is cast into it a thousand years later, and the plural statement of torment at Revelation 20:10 takes in all three; the supplied “are” is not relied on.",
    "The lake is one lake, named the same way in 19:20, 20:10, 20:14, 20:15 and 21:8; it is described as burning after the human dead are cast in, and the torment of human worshippers in 14:9–11 is stated in the terms of 20:10; the human lost cast into it therefore remain under its punishment for ever, which is Inferred, firmly, from the steps of section 8.4.",
    "The lake is called the second death; in the book’s own portrayal the dead exist, stand and are judged, so death does not inherently mean the person’s ceasing.",
    "The judgment is according to works and is graded; the passage does not say the lake is emptied or ended.",
    "The great white throne company is identified with the rest of the dead from the sequence of the chapter, marked as Inferred; the first-resurrection company is distinguished from it by Revelation 20:6, and the Body’s absence is a conclusion from the book’s audience and setting.",
    "The books and the book of life are kept as two functions, and the question of those who believed in the thousand years is left open.",
  ]),
]
